module Core

### Handle to a `suspended routine` — a stackful coroutine. Calling a `suspended routine` creates
### the coroutine (it does not start running) and yields one of these handles. `retrieve!()` drives
### it to completion and returns its value; dropping the handle abandons a coroutine that never
### finished (its cancellation shadow stack tears down whatever it still owned).
###
### v0.2.0: single coroutine, driven synchronously by `retrieve!`. A scheduler/reactor (so a parked
### coroutine makes progress on its own) is the rest of v0.2.0's async work.
record Coroutine[T]
  ### The opaque coroutine handle (rf_coro*).
  secret coro: CPtr
  ### The task control block that holds the produced value (completed by the coroutine's entry thunk).
  secret result: Address

### Drives the coroutine to completion and returns its produced value. Each call to the underlying
### resume runs the body until its next suspend point; we pump until it finishes.
### :returns: The value the suspended routine returned.
routine Coroutine[T].retrieve!() -> ?T
  if me.coro.is_none()
    throw TaskSpawnError()
  danger!
    var status = rf_coro_resume(coro: me.coro)
    while status == 2_u32              # RF_CORO_PARKED — keep pumping until it finishes
      status = rf_coro_resume(coro: me.coro)

    var payload = rf_task_result_payload(task: me.result)
    var value = Hijacked[T](from: payload).extract()
    rf_invalidate(ptr: payload)
    return value

### Teardown: a completed coroutine is freed; one that never finished is abandoned (its shadow
### stack runs, tearing down whatever it still owns). The result task block is always released.
dangerous routine Coroutine[T].$destroy()
  if not me.coro.is_none()
    var status = rf_coro_status_get(coro: me.coro)
    if status == 3_u32                # RF_CORO_COMPLETED
      rf_coro_delete(coro: me.coro)
    else
      rf_coro_abandon(coro: me.coro)
    rf_task_destroy(task: me.result)
  return
